1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,360
 In this lecture, we're going to focus on Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:11,520
 So this service focuses on data collection and data pre-processing.

3
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:18,480
 So there are four key steps that you need to follow to enable data processing through

4
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:21,680
 the data Wrangler service.

5
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:29,000
 So the first one is to import the data into data Wrangler from services such as S3, Athena,

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,660
 and there are 50 plus third-party sources through which you can import data into data

7
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:36,660
 Wrangler.

8
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:39,080
 And you can see all the services that it supports.

9
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:44,960
 You can also do a local upload, maybe an upload CSV file into data Wrangler.

10
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:48,920
 And you can see the screenshot here from data Wrangler.

11
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:55,480
 So here I just imported a data set from the ill-fated ship Titanic.

12
00:00:55,480 --> 00:01:01,460
 And you can see in this data set, you have the passenger class, survived one for

13
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:07,760
 survived and zero for wooden survive, the names of all the passengers, age, ticket,

14
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:09,720
 fare and so on.

15
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:17,440
 So once you have imported this data, the next is to understand the data quality and then generate

16
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:19,800
 data insights.

17
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:24,240
 And this is also called as exploratory data analysis.

18
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:28,400
 And you should remember this from the exam perspective, there's a very good chance that

19
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:36,320
 they might ask about which AWS service is used for exploratory data analysis.

20
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:41,760
 Now let's try and understand better what we do as part of this step.

21
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:49,200
 So now you can see from the screenshot, once we have imported that Titanic data, you can

22
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:53,840
 have a table summary view here and here are all the data points.

23
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,240
 And it shows you a summary view.

24
00:01:56,240 --> 00:02:03,720
 So it will show you the passenger class, it's about 1309, survived, we have about 1309 records.

25
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:07,260
 But if you look at the age, you have only one, zero, four, six.

26
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:12,200
 So you can imagine that there are some data points that are missing.

27
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:16,880
 So that's how you can understand the data quality.

28
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:20,920
 Then the next is exploratory data analysis.

29
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:26,360
 And here you can see, you can generate a histogram and there are other chart types.

30
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:30,080
 You can use bar chart, pie chart and so on.

31
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:36,440
 And here we have on the X axis, we have the six and then we have the color by survive.

32
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:41,480
 So for males and females, what was the percentage that survived?

33
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:47,560
 So this is the percentage for males that survived and this is the percentage for females.

34
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:54,320
 So you can use different chart types to analyze the data, also sometimes called as exploratory

35
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:56,200
 data analysis.

36
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:02,080
 So once you have understood the data quality and generated data insights, the third step

37
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:06,560
 is to transform data more efficiently and analyze.

38
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:12,800
 So here you might want to drop some columns, handle outliers and let's say, so we're trying

39
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:15,800
 to predict what kind of passenger survived.

40
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:22,320
 Maybe you don't need a column with address of the people as that might not be a factor

41
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:24,280
 in whether they survive or not.

42
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:29,600
 So what you can do is you can use either custom transformation or some prebuilt transformation

43
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:34,520
 to drop the column or handle outliers, handle missing data.

44
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:37,800
 So those are the capabilities that are provided by data handler.

45
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:43,440
 So you can see here, so you can do the transformation either through prebuilt capabilities

46
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:46,440
 or custom transformations.

47
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:52,720
 So once you have transformed the data, then you can export the data either to S3 or SageMaker

48
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:59,880
 feature store and we'll discuss about SageMaker feature store next or you can also use SageMaker

49
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:01,200
 pipelines.

50
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:05,360
 So hopefully the Amazon SageMaker data wrangler capabilities are clear to you.

51
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:07,200
 I'll see you in the next lecture, thank you.

